  <h1>Architecture marketing statistics and costs in 2026</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The architecture industry has no shortage of benchmarks and almost no numbers. Surveys will
    tell you what percentage of revenue your peers say they spend. Almost nobody publishes what
    a client actually costs to go out and get.
  </p>

  <p>
    So this page carries both. The verified industry data on the market you are selling into,
    the live cost of buying attention as of October 2026, and the part that usually stays inside
    agency dashboards: the real cost per lead and cost per booked consultation from 21
    architecture firm ad accounts we run. Every number here has a source under it, and where
    the source is us, we say so and show the sample.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>An architecture firm buying its own client flow in 2026 is looking at about $50 in Meta ad spend per lead and about $148 per booked consultation for the median firm, based on 2,419 leads across 21 US architecture firm ad accounts between September 2025 and September 2026. Google search clicks on architecture terms run about $4 to $18 each before anyone fills in anything, and $10 on "architect near me" itself. Agency retainers commonly run $2,500 to $12,000 a month. Set against architecture fees of 8 to 15 percent of construction cost, one signed residential project usually covers a year of all of it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The market you are marketing into</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the weather, because it explains why referral flow got thin. The <a href="https://www.aia.org/resource-center/abi-august-2026-architecture-firm-billings-continue-decline" rel="nofollow">AIA and Deltek
    Architecture Billings Index came in at 47.2 for August 2026</a>, up a hair from 46.6 in July.
    Anything under 50 means more firms reported billings falling than rising, and by the <a href="https://www.aia.org/resource-center/abi-july-2026-architecture-firm-billings-remain-weak" rel="nofollow">AIA's
    own count</a> the slump has now run nearly three and a half years, the longest in the index's
    history.
  </p>
  <p>
    Underneath the headline, the pattern from early summer held. The project inquiries index read
    50.8 in August while design contracts came in at 48.3. So people are still asking. Fewer of
    those conversations are turning into signed work, and the asking is cooling too: inquiries
    read 56.1 back in June.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which kind of firm you are changes the reading. Firms with a commercial or industrial
    specialization scored 50.4, the only specialization above 50 in August. Multifamily
    residential sat at 47.8, institutional at 47.0 and mixed practice at 42.5. The index does
    not break out single-family homes, so a firm designing private residences has no direct line
    here, and the closest proxy is the search demand further down this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Region spreads it even wider. On three-month averages the Midwest posted 50.7, its
    first growth in nearly a year, the West 49.8, the South 46.1 and the Northeast 40.7, its
    lowest since 2020. And the people running these firms are getting gloomier: 31 percent of firm
    leaders expected billings to rise in the second quarter, and only 25 percent expect it for
    the fourth.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.deltek.com/en/architecture-and-engineering/clarity" rel="nofollow">Deltek's 47th annual Clarity study</a>, built on 896 firms reporting 2025 data, shows what that
    feels like in a business development log. Firms submitted 32 percent more proposals than the
    year before, while the total value of those proposals grew only 12 percent. More chasing,
    smaller prizes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median win rate across the industry landed at 49 percent, and median capture rate slipped
    3.8 points to 44.4 percent. Architecture firms specifically gained five points, to a 45
    percent win rate, which still sits under the all-industry median.
  </p>

